\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The equal-weight counting exponent}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{The general cited statement is \ref{fdip-00G9}. The
structure of \ref{fdip-00EX}, the regular harmonic
structure of \ref{fdip-00EZ}, and the measure of
\ref{fdip-00EY} with #{\mu_i=\frac{1}{3}} satisfy the
hypotheses stated for
\citet{Theorem 4.6}{kigami1996kokyuroku}. That statement
gives, for both the Dirichlet and the Neumann counting
functions #{\rho_*},
##{0<\liminf_{\Lambda\to\infty}\rho_*(\Lambda)/\Lambda^{d_s/2}\le\limsup_{\Lambda\to\infty}\rho_*(\Lambda)/\Lambda^{d_s/2}<\infty,}
where #{d_s} is the unique positive solution of
#{\sum_{i=1}^{3}(r_i\mu_i)^{d_s/2}=1}. For these weights
#{d_s=2\log 3/\log 5}. The logarithmic counting exponent
exists and equals #{d_s}:
##{\lim_{\Lambda\to\infty}2\log\rho_*(\Lambda)/\log\Lambda=d_s.}
The cited statement attributes the two-sided bound to
Theorem 2.4 of Kigami and Lapidus, Communications in
Mathematical Physics, volume 158 (1993). That article was
not opened. The equal weights fall under the lattice clause
of the 1996 statement, so the ratio
#{\rho_*(\Lambda)/\Lambda^{d_s/2}} is not given a constant
limit.}

\proof{
\p{Post-critical finiteness is \ref{fdip-00EX}. The
resistances #{r_i=\frac{3}{5}} and the trace equality are
\ref{fdip-00EZ}, and #{\frac{3}{5}<1}. Full support and the
absence of finite atoms are \ref{fdip-00EY}. These are the
hypotheses printed before the cited theorem. The cited
formula is #{\gamma_i=\sqrt{r_i\mu_i}}. Here
#{\gamma_i=\sqrt{1/5}} for each #{i}, so the equation reads
#{3\cdot(1/5)^{d_s/2}=1}, hence #{5^{d_s/2}=3} and
#{d_s=2\log 3/\log 5}. The three logarithms
#{\log\gamma_i} agree, so they generate a cyclic group and
the lattice clause applies.}

\p{The displayed liminf is positive and the limsup is finite,
so there exist #{\Lambda_0} and constants #{0<c_1\le c_2}
such that
#{c_1\le\rho_*(\Lambda)/\Lambda^{d_s/2}\le c_2} whenever
#{\Lambda>\Lambda_0}. Taking logarithms leaves a bounded
remainder added to #{\tfrac{d_s}{2}\log\Lambda}. Dividing by
#{\log\Lambda} sends that quotient to #{\tfrac{d_s}{2}}.
Multiplication by #{2} is the displayed limit. The same
algebra applies to both boundary conditions named in the
cited statement.}
}
